From: OpenAI-assisted development
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] dir842-asic: make router -> bridge restart self-cleaning

The init script still treats router -> bridge as a one-way runtime transition:
after router mode has run gw_prog(), TEACR/L3 state remains programmed and L2
aging stays frozen until reboot.

That restriction predates fabric_gw_rearm.  Current v1.4.1 already proves that
a level-3 fabric reset is safe in bridge role when fabric_gw_rearm=0: the RX
stall recovery uses exactly that combination and returns the bridge to a healthy
L2-only datapath without re-running gw_prog().

Set the role-specific fabric_gw_rearm value first (already done by asic_up), then
run level-3 reset for BOTH roles.  Router mode still follows it with gw_prog;
bridge mode deliberately does not.  This clears stale router tables/frozen
aging on a router -> bridge `restart` and makes the role transition symmetric.

--- a/files/target/linux/realtek/base-files/etc/init.d/dir842-asic
+++ b/files/target/linux/realtek/base-files/etc/init.d/dir842-asic
@@ -100,11 +100,12 @@ wait_netif_mac() {
 # is NOT: nothing in bridge role clears TEACR bit0, and no userspace knob exists
 # to unfreeze L2 aging, so a router->bridge flip by `restart` leaves aging FROZEN
 # and the router L3 tables programmed while the log below claims "L2 aging left
-# ENABLED". REBOOT after switching router->bridge.
+# ENABLED".  v1.4.1 has fabric_gw_rearm, so asic_program now runs the safe
+# level-3 reset in bridge role too (with gw re-arm disabled), which clears that
+# stale router state before bridge operation resumes.
 asic_role() {
 	local r
 	r="$(uci -q get dir842.asic.role)"
@@ -120,21 +121,21 @@ asic_program() {
 	swconfig dev switch0 set apply 1 2>/dev/null
 	sleep 1
 	# ★ MEASURED-REQUIRED (see header). Level 3 = engine reinit + CPUICR soft-reset +
 	# full fabric reset (SIRR FULL_RST -> swcore clock cycle -> MEMCR SRAM re-init ->
-	# 153-reg restore), and it re-arms the ASIC gw tables in-kernel, which is why
-	# gw_prog must follow it.
-	# ⚠ ROUTER ONLY. Level 3 is "+full fabric reset" and, per this file's header,
-	# "re-arms the ASIC gw tables in-kernel" — it restores the L3 datapath AND
-	# the TEACR aging freeze even when gw_prog is skipped (verified 2026-08-23:
-	# routes and MSCR/SWTCR0/TEATCR came back with gw_prog gated off). Running it
-	# in bridge role recreates the exact condition the bridge path avoids.
-	# The header's "without it a fresh boot forwards NOTHING" was measured in
-	# ROUTER mode; in bridge role the ASIC is left in its loader L2 state and
-	# plain switching works (measured: association, broadcast/multicast and
-	# client ARP all traverse).
-	if [ "$ROLE" = "router" ]; then
-		echo 3 > /sys/module/rtl819x/parameters/fabric_reset 2>/dev/null
-		sleep 3
-	fi
+	# 153-reg restore).  Since v1.3 the dangerous half of level 3 — gw_rearm() —
+	# is independently gated by fabric_gw_rearm.  asic_up() sets that to 1 for a
+	# router and 0 for a bridge BEFORE entering here.
+	#
+	# Therefore run the reset in BOTH roles.  In router role gw_rearm/gw_prog
+	# rebuild the L3/L4 tables.  In bridge role the same full reset runs with
+	# gw_rearm SKIPPED, which is the already hardware-validated RX-stall recovery
+	# path.  Besides making bridge startup deterministic, this clears stale
+	# router tables + frozen L2 aging when changing router -> bridge via restart.
+	echo 3 > /sys/module/rtl819x/parameters/fabric_reset 2>/dev/null
+	sleep 3
+
 	# read side-effect: gw_prog() programs the whole L3/L4 datapath. NOTE this WIPES
 	# the ASIC L2 tables, so the warm-up must come after.
 	# gw_prog: L3/L4 datapath + TEACR aging freeze. ROUTER ONLY (see asic_role).
